<h2>The statutory scheme: two provisions, two levels</h2>
<p>The BNSS splits the transfer power by geography. <strong>Section 446</strong> vests in the Supreme Court the power to direct, whenever it is made to appear that an order under the section is <em>expedient for the ends of justice</em>, that a particular case or appeal be transferred from one High Court to another High Court, or from a criminal court subordinate to one High Court to another criminal court of equal or superior jurisdiction subordinate to a different High Court. <strong>Section 447</strong> — the successor to Section 407 CrPC — houses the High Court&rsquo;s power to transfer cases <em>within</em> the State: from one sessions division to another, or from one subordinate court to another, on grounds the provision spells out, including that a fair and impartial inquiry or trial cannot be had, that a question of law of unusual difficulty is involved, or that transfer is otherwise expedient for the ends of justice.</p>

<table class="law">
