  <tr><td>Section 447 BNSS (407 CrPC)</td><td>High Court</td><td>Within the State: between sessions divisions and subordinate courts</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Sections below</td><td>Sessions Judge</td><td>Within the sessions division</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>What persuades the Supreme Court — and what does not</h2>
<p>The phrase &ldquo;expedient for the ends of justice&rdquo; is deliberately open, but decades of transfer practice have given it working content. The Court is moved by a demonstrable threat to the fairness of the trial itself; it is unmoved by the ordinary frictions of litigating away from home.</p>

<div class="compare">
  <div class="col old"><strong>Grounds that succeed</strong><br>Credible threats to the life or safety of a party or witnesses; a communally or politically surcharged atmosphere around the trial; an accused so locally powerful that witnesses cannot depose freely; a litigant who demonstrably cannot appear or defend in the distant forum; serious apprehension, on objective material, that justice will not be seen to be done.</div>
  <div class="arrow">↔</div>
  <div class="col new"><strong>Grounds that fail</strong><br>Distance, expense or inconvenience standing alone; bald allegations of bias against the presiding judge; apprehensions unsupported by affidavit-level particulars; transfer sought as a delaying device after the trial has substantially progressed.</div>
</div>

<p>Matrimonial prosecutions occupy a special corner of this practice. Where a wife prosecutes or defends proceedings far from her residence, the Court weighs the relative hardship of the spouses with care — but even there, the modern availability of <strong>evidence by video-conferencing</strong> and appearance through counsel has tempered the readiness to transfer. The BNSS&rsquo;s express embrace of audio-video means for appearance and evidence gives respondents a standard answer to hardship-based petitions: the witness need not travel at all. Petitioners must therefore explain why remote participation is inadequate on their facts, not merely inconvenient.</p>

<h2>Procedure before the Supreme Court</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Petition and affidavit.</strong> A transfer petition on the criminal side is filed setting out the case history and the precise facts said to imperil justice. An application by a party interested must be supported by affidavit or affirmation; the Attorney-General alone is exempt from that requirement.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Notice and interim orders.</strong> The Court issues notice to the opposite party and the State concerned, and may stay the trial in the meantime. Without a stay, the trial court proceeds, and the petition can be rendered infructuous by an intervening judgment.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Hearing.</strong> The question is not guilt or innocence but venue. The Court looks for objective material — police reports, orders recording courtroom incidents, documented threats — rather than rhetoric.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Order.</strong> If allowed, the case moves to a court of equal or superior jurisdiction in the transferee State, which proceeds from the stage already reached unless the Court directs otherwise. If dismissed as frivolous or vexatious, the Court may direct the applicant to pay compensation to those who opposed the petition.</div>
</div>

<h2>A working checklist</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p>For petitioners: plead specific incidents with dates, documents and FIR or complaint numbers; generalities about fear do not survive the affidavit requirement.</p>
  <p>Address the video-conferencing answer squarely — explain why remote evidence or exemption from appearance cannot cure the prejudice alleged.</p>
  <p>Seek interim stay at filing, and keep the trial court informed of the pendency of the petition.</p>
  <p>For respondents: meet the petition with equally specific material — proof of the petitioner&rsquo;s participation in the trial, orders showing no untoward incident, and the offer of procedural accommodations in the existing court.</p>
  <p>Remember the cost provision: the compensation clause for frivolous petitions is invoked rarely, but its presence disciplines the pleading.</p>
</div>

<div class="note">Transfer petitions on the civil side — including transfer of matrimonial suits between Family Courts in different States under Section 25 of the Code of Civil Procedure — follow a separate regime already covered elsewhere on this site. The present note is confined to criminal transfers under the BNSS.</div>
